MANAGEMENT OF AI PRODUCTS


SESSION 1 — INTRODUCTION TO AI PRODUCTS AND THEIR EMERGENCE
Defining AI Products
•	Core Concept: An AI product should use AI to materially improve a decision, workflow, or user outcome. AI can be the product itself, a decision layer, or an invisible operational engine.
•	Key Details & Data: Do not label something “AI-powered” merely because it contains a model. Evidence should include task-completion improvement, recommendation relevance, real-world accuracy, and changed user behaviour.
•	Real-World Example: Google Maps uses AI for live traffic prediction. GitHub Copilot puts AI directly inside the developer workflow.
Traditional Software vs AI Products
•	Core Concept: The key question is not simply whether AI exists in the software. The PM must establish whether AI materially improves the product experience.
•	Key Details & Data: AI product quality appears through workflow friction, trust, repeat use, relevance, accuracy, and behavioural change.
•	Real-World Example: Google Maps traffic prediction demonstrates how AI continuously influences the user's navigation experience.
Rule-Based vs Learning Systems
•	Core Concept: Product teams must make the trade-off between predefined rules and learning-based behaviour explicit.
•	Key Details & Data: Teams should clarify review quality, escalation paths, resourcing, and learning discipline rather than leaving the core trade-off vague.
•	Real-World Example: The slides reference Microsoft under Satya Nadella as an example of organizational learning discipline.
Prediction, Recommendation and Generative AI
•	Core Concept: AI products can be classified according to whether they predict/classify, recommend/rank, generate, or combine these capabilities.
•	Key Details & Data: Start with the operational problem. Ask exactly what the model must predict, rank, classify, recommend, or generate. Convert the business problem into a measurable product decision.
•	Real-World Example: Support-ticket routing and credit-risk screening both use AI, but have very different product and risk requirements.
________________________________________
CORE AI PRODUCT TYPES
Prediction Products
•	Core Concept: Prediction products use AI to predict or classify an outcome that supports a product or business decision.
•	Key Details & Data: The prediction must connect to an explicit, measurable target. PMs should avoid vague statements such as simply “use AI.”
•	Real-World Example: Support-ticket routing and credit-risk screening.
Recommendation Systems
•	Core Concept: Recommendation products rank or select options that are relevant to the user's job.
•	Key Details & Data: The AI product type must match the user need. Success should be tested through the primary user job, repeat usage, task success, and user trust.
•	Real-World Example: Netflix recommendations and ranking.
Generative AI Products
•	Core Concept: Generative products create outputs as part of the user's workflow.
•	Key Details & Data: Product quality must be judged by task success, repeat use, workflow fit, and whether users trust the generated output enough to act on it.
•	Real-World Example: ChatGPT as a generative work companion.
Hybrid AI Products
•	Core Concept: Hybrid products combine prediction, recommendation, generation, or other AI modes.
•	Key Details & Data: Combining modes is useful only when each supports the user job. Mixing categories carelessly creates unclear value propositions, confused UX, and weak measurement.
•	Real-World Example: A product can combine Netflix-style recommendation logic with generative capabilities.
⭐ Exam Point
Choose the AI product type based on the user job, not based on which AI technology appears most impressive.
