WHY AI PRODUCTS EMERGED
Digital Data Abundance
•	Core Concept: Large amounts of digital behavioural and operational data made modern AI products increasingly viable.
•	Key Details & Data: Data alone is insufficient. Teams need coverage, completeness, freshness, consent assumptions, event schemas, and reliable event-quality checks.
•	Real-World Example: Amazon clickstream pipelines illustrate how user activity can create data for AI-driven product decisions.
Cloud and Compute Accessibility
•	Core Concept: Easier access to cloud infrastructure and compute reduced barriers to building and operating AI products.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs should identify what actually made a product viable: data availability, cheaper compute, better models, or better distribution.
•	Real-World Example: AWS and cloud ML infrastructure.
Model Breakthroughs and APIs
•	Core Concept: Better models and accessible APIs allowed companies to introduce AI without developing every capability internally.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must decide what to fund, delay, own, borrow, or treat merely as a feature. AI enthusiasm alone is not sufficient justification.
•	Real-World Example: OpenAI and other API-first model platforms.
Improved Interfaces and User Familiarity
•	Core Concept: AI adoption also became possible because interfaces became easier to use and customers became more comfortable interacting with AI.
•	Key Details & Data: Commercial readiness depends on data exhaust, cloud access, model quality, API availability, interface familiarity, and low integration friction.
•	Real-World Example: Cloud ML infrastructure and API-first AI platforms lowered adoption barriers.
⭐ Why AI Products Became Viable
Data + Compute + Better Models + APIs + Familiar Interfaces + Lower Integration Friction
The slides warn against the simplistic idea that “AI suddenly became possible.” The commercial environment changed alongside the technology.
