INDIAN MARKET CONTEXT
India as an AI Product Environment
•	Core Concept: AI products in India must account for scale, language diversity, price sensitivity, digital infrastructure, and trust expectations.
•	Key Details & Data: Relevant evidence includes acquisition cost, regional usage mix, vernacular adoption, payment conversion, and operational reliability by segment.
•	Real-World Example: UPI represents digital public infrastructure. Swiggy represents multilingual, high-frequency digital commerce.
Multilingual Demand
•	Core Concept: Language diversity directly affects product design and AI behaviour in India.
•	Key Details & Data: Teams should test multiple prompt variants and examine error rate, refusal quality, correction burden, fallback behaviour, and human override requirements.
•	Real-World Example: AI products operating across Indian markets may need vernacular support rather than an English-first global design.
Global Playbooks vs Indian Reality
•	Core Concept: A global AI product cannot automatically be transplanted into India unchanged.
•	Key Details & Data: Onboarding, pricing, language support, operational realities, trust, and payment behaviour may require adaptation.
•	Real-World Example: The slides ask students to take a global AI product and determine what would need to change for credible operation in India.
________________________________________
CONSUMER, BUSINESS AND REGULATION
Consumer Behaviour Shift
•	Core Concept: Consumers increasingly interact with AI not only for information but also for guidance and decisions.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must ask: Who benefits from the AI decision? Who carries the risk if it is wrong? Who requires explanation or control?
•	Real-World Example: Amazon recommendation economics illustrates AI guiding consumer choices.
Business Model Shift Toward Decision Layers
•	Core Concept: AI increasingly becomes a decision layer within a business rather than only a standalone feature.
•	Key Details & Data: Optimizing convenience or growth without considering trust can lead to regulatory attention and downstream harm.
•	Real-World Example: Amazon recommendations influence product discovery and purchase behaviour.
Regulation
•	Core Concept: Regulators care when AI decisions create material consequences for users or businesses.
•	Key Details & Data: Product managers must balance user value, business value, governance, explanation, and control.
•	Real-World Example: The slides contrast recommendation economics with the EU AI Act and financial-services compliance pressures.
Trust and Transparency
•	Core Concept: Users must trust that an AI product will not mislead, overstep, or conceal risk.
•	Key Details & Data: Trust affects adoption, support cost, and regulatory exposure. PMs must determine what must be explained, reviewed, overridden, or constrained.
•	Real-World Example: High-impact AI decisions may require user verification or human review.
________________________________________
FOUNDATIONS FOR LATER AI PRODUCT MANAGEMENT
Human-in-the-Loop
•	Core Concept: AI does not automatically mean full automation. Some decisions require AI assistance plus human review.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must decide which decisions deserve automation, assisted review, or mandatory human approval. Review policies need thresholds, responsibilities, and turnaround targets.
•	Real-World Example: Stripe Radar review queues and Uber Eats fraud operations.
Product Management Foundation
•	Core Concept: AI product management connects opportunity → design → data → metrics → scale → governance.
•	Key Details & Data: Before approving AI, the PM should ask: “What product decision is AI actually improving?”
•	Real-World Example: The slides compare Duolingo personalized learning with Uber ETA, pricing, and marketplace-balancing systems.
Swiggy Case Study
•	Core Concept: A single product can contain several AI systems simultaneously.
•	Key Details & Data: Swiggy combines recommendation, demand prediction, and ETA estimation. AI is partly visible to users and partly invisible in operations. Business value appears through conversion, basket size, logistics efficiency, and trust.
•	Real-World Example: Swiggy's recommendation and ETA systems demonstrate how several AI capabilities combine within one customer journey.
⭐ Session 1 Takeaway
Before building AI, ask:
Does AI make this workflow better, faster, safer, or more defensible?
The biggest trap is confusing technical possibility with product necessity.
