SESSION 2 — ROLE OF THE AI PRODUCT MANAGER & AI PRODUCT STACK
Role of the AI Product Manager
•	Core Concept: The AI PM owns product judgment but does not personally own every technical decision.
•	Key Details & Data: AI PMs own problem framing, success logic, prioritization, and launch criteria. They co-own workflow quality, risk trade-offs, and escalation paths. They influence model, data, and platform decisions.
•	Real-World Example: The slides compare GitHub Copilot product leadership with Google Maps navigation product decisions.
AI PM vs Traditional PM
•	Core Concept: AI PM work involves greater uncertainty across models, data, technical feasibility, trust, and quality.
•	Key Details & Data: The PM must establish clear decision rights, escalation ownership, launch criteria, and strong business-to-technical translation.
•	Real-World Example: GitHub Copilot demonstrates product decisions involving AI quality, user workflow, and technical capabilities.
Decision Rights and Accountability
•	Core Concept: Every major AI trade-off needs a clear decider, reviewer, and escalation owner.
•	Key Details & Data: Separate product decisions, engineering decisions, and shared escalations. Weak accountability produces slow meetings and unclear responsibility.
•	Real-World Example: Before launch, ownership gaps should be identified rather than discovered after failure.
Business–Technical Translation
•	Core Concept: AI PMs translate business needs into technical requirements and technical constraints into product realities.
•	Key Details & Data: Business stakeholders need to understand latency, data limitations, and quality. Technical teams need to understand customer pain, revenue logic, and trust risk.
•	Real-World Example: If business and engineering leave a discussion with different definitions of the product, the translation has failed.
⭐ AI PM Ownership
Own: Problem framing, success logic, prioritization, launch criteria.
Co-own: Workflow quality, risk trade-offs, escalation.
Influence: Data, model and platform choices.
