AI PRODUCT STACK
Data Layer
•	Core Concept: Data provides the signals that support the AI product.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must examine event schema, consent assumptions, freshness, downstream use, coverage, completeness, and event quality.
•	Real-World Example: Amazon clickstream pipelines.
Model Layer
•	Core Concept: The model transforms data into predictions, rankings, classifications, or other AI outputs.
•	Key Details & Data: The model should not be mistaken for the whole product. PMs must identify whether the bottleneck is actually the data, model, API, or UX.
•	Real-World Example: Netflix recommendation stack and Stripe fraud-detection stack.
API and Application Logic
•	Core Concept: APIs and application logic connect AI capabilities with the wider product workflow.
•	Key Details & Data: Poor integration can destroy value even when the model itself performs well. Teams must avoid relying on signals that are partial, stale, or unreliable.
•	Real-World Example: Spotify event instrumentation is used to illustrate decisions around useful versus noisy data collection.
User Experience Layer
•	Core Concept: The UX determines how users encounter, understand, trust, and act on AI output.
•	Key Details & Data: Product evidence includes data freshness, model lift, API reliability, UX adoption, and support tickets linked to particular stack layers.
•	Real-World Example: A technically strong fraud model can still fail as a product if the surrounding workflow is confusing.
⭐ AI Product Stack
DATA → MODEL → API / APPLICATION LOGIC → UX
A common mistake is treating the model as the entire product.
________________________________________
WORKING ACROSS FUNCTIONS
Cross-Functional AI Product Development
•	Core Concept: AI products require coordinated decisions across product, engineering, data, design, business, risk, and governance functions.
•	Key Details & Data: The PM's role is not to replace specialists. It is to align decisions, trade-offs, evidence, ownership, and escalation.
•	Real-World Example: Fraud and recommendation products require technical, product, operational, and risk teams to work together.
Human Review and Escalation
•	Core Concept: Human review should exist only where it creates meaningful value.
•	Key Details & Data: Define routing thresholds, reviewer responsibilities, turnaround targets, and situations requiring mandatory human approval.
•	Real-World Example: Stripe Radar review queues.
________________________________________
AI PM DELIVERABLES
Decision Artifacts
•	Core Concept: AI PMs convert ambiguity into concrete, reviewable artifacts.
•	Key Details & Data: Useful artifacts include written recommendations, trade-offs, evidence checks, data requirements, review policies, and launch criteria.
•	Real-World Example: A data requirements brief can specify event schema, consent, freshness, and downstream use.
Launch Discipline
•	Core Concept: AI products require explicit evidence before launch or scale.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs should define success measures, failure thresholds, escalation ownership, and launch criteria early.
•	Real-World Example: A product should not scale merely because model performance looks promising if API reliability or UX adoption remains poor.
⭐ Session 2 Takeaway
An AI PM does not need to be the data scientist, engineer, designer, or risk officer.
The PM's job is to ensure that:
the right problem + right evidence + right stack + right people + clear decision rights = a viable AI product.
