SESSION 3 — AI PRODUCT STRATEGY & OPPORTUNITY IDENTIFICATION
AI Opportunity Identification
•	Core Concept: AI strategy starts by identifying problems worth solving, not by searching for places to insert AI.
•	Key Details & Data: Opportunities should be assessed using customer pain, workflow fit, business value, trust implications, and feasibility.
•	Real-World Example: A bank may identify AI opportunities in support, fraud triage, sales assistance, and knowledge retrieval.
Value vs Feasibility
•	Core Concept: A good AI opportunity must offer meaningful value and have a plausible path to execution.
•	Key Details & Data: Evaluate expected business upside, data readiness, operational constraints, model realism, and time-to-value.
•	Real-World Example: Fraud scoring in fintech versus demand forecasting in commerce.
⭐ Value–Feasibility Logic
	High Feasibility	Low Feasibility
High Value	Strong candidate	Attractive but difficult
Low Value	Easy but weak opportunity	Reject/avoid
The slides stress that organizations often select easy but low-value projects or high-value projects with no realistic execution path.
________________________________________
BUILD, BUY OR API
Commodity vs Strategic Capability
•	Core Concept: Decide which AI capabilities create strategic differentiation and which can be sourced externally.
•	Key Details & Data: Examine market pull, data readiness, distribution logic, strategic importance, and the reason the investment should happen now.
•	Real-World Example: Amazon recommendations illustrate a capability that can be strategically important.
Vendor Speed vs Dependency
•	Core Concept: External vendors and APIs can accelerate launch but create dependency.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must decide what to own, borrow, buy, partner for, or treat as a feature.
•	Real-World Example: OpenAI API integration versus in-house fraud/ranking capabilities at large platforms.
Control and Differentiation
•	Core Concept: Build internally when ownership meaningfully contributes to strategic advantage.
•	Key Details & Data: Consider strategic importance, switching cost, vendor dependency, compliance fit, time-to-market, and ownership of core IP.
•	Real-World Example: Large platforms may develop their own ranking or fraud capabilities when these are core to differentiation.
Cost and Governance
•	Core Concept: Technical capability does not automatically create a commercially viable product.
•	Key Details & Data: Unit economics should link pricing, usage, model cost, support cost, and gross margin.
•	Real-World Example: The slides reference OpenAI API pricing and Midjourney/Cursor packaging.
⭐ Build–Buy–API Decision
Ask:
Is this capability commodity or strategically worth owning?
________________________________________
PLATFORM VS FEATURE
Feature Logic
•	Core Concept: An AI capability can initially solve one narrow problem as a feature.
•	Key Details & Data: Teams should prove value in the first workflow before expanding scope.
•	Real-World Example: Canva AI features operate inside a broader product ecosystem.
Platform Logic
•	Core Concept: A platform supports reusable AI capabilities across multiple workflows or use cases.
•	Key Details & Data: Evidence should include cross-workflow applicability, ecosystem pull, extensibility, repeat use, and adoption in the first use case.
•	Real-World Example: ChatGPT platform expansion.
Premature Platform Ambition
•	Core Concept: A team should not claim to be building a platform before proving deep and repeatable value.
•	Key Details & Data: Platform stories fail when teams have not yet earned depth, habit, or repeatable value in one workflow.
•	Real-World Example: The slides ask whether an AI idea should remain a feature or grow into a platform.
⭐ Feature → Platform Rule
First prove one workflow. Then prove repeat use. Then prove cross-workflow applicability. Only then justify platform ambition.
________________________________________
JOBS-TO-BE-DONE (JTBD) FOR AI PRODUCTS
JTBD
•	Core Concept: Start with the progress the customer wants to make, not with the AI technology available.
•	Key Details & Data: Ask: “What progress is the customer trying to make, and where does AI reduce effort, anxiety, or delay?”
•	Real-World Example: Duolingo learning support and Stripe fraud prevention for merchants.
Functional and Emotional Jobs
•	Core Concept: Customer jobs can include both functional outcomes and emotional concerns such as anxiety.
•	Key Details & Data: Examine decision context, switching triggers, anxiety sources, desired progress, and whether AI materially improves the outcome.
•	Real-World Example: A merchant using fraud prevention wants to reduce fraudulent transactions but may also want confidence that legitimate customers will not be wrongly blocked.
Solution-First Thinking
•	Core Concept: Starting with an AI model and searching afterward for a customer problem produces weak strategy.
•	Key Details & Data: JTBD keeps teams focused on customer progress before selecting the technology.
•	Real-World Example: Instead of asking “Where can we use generative AI?”, ask “What customer job currently creates significant effort, anxiety, or delay?”
