SESSION 5 — DESIGNING AI PRODUCTS: USER & MODEL THINKING
User-Centred AI Product Design
•	Core Concept: AI product design starts with the user's workflow, not with the model.
•	Key Details & Data: Identify the user's intent, friction, AI intervention point, fallback, and success signal. AI should shorten or improve the job rather than add unnecessary interface steps.
•	Real-World Example: Notion AI illustrates AI embedded within an existing user workflow.
Workflow Before Interface
•	Core Concept: Designing a chatbot or AI button is not the same as designing an AI product.
•	Key Details & Data: A workflow wireframe should show entry point → user intent → AI behaviour → fallback → success signal.
•	Real-World Example: Notion AI and Microsoft Copilot show different ways AI can appear within work.
Quick Recall
Do not design the AI box. Design the user's workflow.
________________________________________
MODEL THINKING FOR PRODUCT MANAGERS
Model Capability vs User Need
•	Core Concept: Model capability must be translated into useful product behaviour.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs do not need to own model engineering. They need to understand what the model can do reliably, where it fails, and what those limitations mean for users.
•	Real-World Example: AI assistants demonstrate why technically impressive output may still fail when it does not fit the workflow.
Probabilistic Behaviour
•	Core Concept: AI outputs can be uncertain rather than perfectly deterministic.
•	Key Details & Data: Product design must account for uncertainty, confidence, error, and variation in model output.
•	Real-World Example: AI-generated outputs may require fallback or user correction when confidence is insufficient.
Confidence and User Control
•	Core Concept: Users need appropriate control when AI outputs are uncertain or consequential.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must decide where to show confidence, where users can correct output, and where humans should review decisions.
•	Real-World Example: High-impact AI workflows may require manual override rather than full automation.
________________________________________
FAILURE-AWARE DESIGN
Designing for AI Failure
•	Core Concept: AI failure must be designed into the product from the beginning.
•	Key Details & Data: Product design should specify fallback behaviour, user correction, escalation, and recovery.
•	Real-World Example: A low-confidence AI output can be routed to human review instead of being automatically accepted.
Trust
•	Core Concept: Trust comes from reliable behaviour and understandable recovery, not from pretending AI is always correct.
•	Key Details & Data: Users should understand what the system can do, when uncertainty matters, and how they can recover from errors.
•	Real-World Example: AI copilots need correction and fallback mechanisms when generated output is wrong.
Human-in-the-Loop Design
•	Core Concept: Human involvement should be deliberately placed where human judgment creates value.
•	Key Details & Data: Decide which decisions can be automated, which need assisted review, and which require mandatory human approval.
•	Real-World Example: Review queues can route uncertain cases to people.
Session 5 Takeaway
The biggest design failure is a clever AI interface without clear decisions about:
Workflow + Confidence + Control + Human Review + Failure Recovery
