SESSION 6 — DATA FUNDAMENTALS FOR PRODUCT MANAGERS
Data as a Product Input
•	Core Concept: AI product quality depends on the quality and usefulness of the underlying signals.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs must understand what data exists, where it comes from, how fresh it is, and whether it represents the product problem correctly.
•	Real-World Example: Amazon clickstream pipelines illustrate behavioural data flowing into AI product decisions.
Data Requirements
•	Core Concept: Teams need an explicit data requirements brief before assuming data is ready.
•	Key Details & Data: The brief should cover event schema, consent assumptions, freshness, downstream use, coverage, completeness, and event quality.
•	Real-World Example: Spotify event instrumentation demonstrates product events being captured as useful signals.
________________________________________
DATA QUALITY
Coverage and Completeness
•	Core Concept: Data must adequately represent the behaviour or decision the AI product is supposed to support.
•	Key Details & Data: Missing segments or incomplete events can weaken the product even when the model itself is sophisticated.
•	Real-World Example: Recommendation systems require sufficiently complete user interaction signals.
Freshness
•	Core Concept: Data must arrive quickly enough for the product decision being made.
•	Key Details & Data: Stale data can produce technically valid but operationally useless predictions.
•	Real-World Example: Real-time marketplace or ETA products depend heavily on fresh signals.
Signal Quality
•	Core Concept: A model cannot compensate indefinitely for weak or misleading input signals.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs should question signal quality before debating models. Data assumptions should be challenged early.
•	Real-World Example: Behavioural events must accurately represent what users are actually doing.
Exam Point
The PDF's key message is:
Do not debate models before you can explain the quality of the signal feeding them.
________________________________________
DATA COLLECTION & INSTRUMENTATION
Event Instrumentation
•	Core Concept: Instrumentation defines which user or system events the product captures.
•	Key Details & Data: PMs should distinguish useful events from data that is noisy, invasive, or operationally useless.
•	Real-World Example: Spotify event instrumentation.
Data Pipelines
•	Core Concept: Data must move reliably from its source to the systems that need it.
•	Key Details & Data: Having data somewhere is not enough. The correct data must arrive at the correct point and at the correct time.
•	Real-World Example: Marketplace and clickstream pipelines illustrate the operational importance of data movement.
Consent and Downstream Use
•	Core Concept: Data collection must account for consent and intended downstream usage.
•	Key Details & Data: A data requirements brief should explicitly state consent assumptions and how captured data will be used.
•	Real-World Example: User-event collection for personalization must be linked to a clear product purpose.
________________________________________
BIAS AND REPRESENTATIVENESS
Data Representation
•	Core Concept: AI performance depends on whether the data represents the population and situations the product will encounter.
•	Key Details & Data: Aggregate performance can hide weaknesses in important user segments.
•	Real-World Example: A product can perform well overall while performing poorly for a specific segment.
Data Assumptions
•	Core Concept: PMs should treat assumptions about data availability and quality as product risks.
•	Key Details & Data: Challenge assumptions about completeness, freshness, coverage, and reliability before investing heavily downstream.
•	Real-World Example: Weak event coverage can undermine a recommendation system regardless of model sophistication.
Session 6 Takeaway
Data availability ≠ data readiness.
Think:
Source → Signal → Quality → Coverage → Freshness → Consent → Pipeline → Product Decision
