SESSION 7 — AI PRODUCT LIFECYCLE
Session 7 treats lifecycle management as an operating system, not merely a sequence of project stages.
PROBLEM & DISCOVERY GATE
Problem Framing
•	Core Concept: Lifecycle discipline starts by confirming that the team is solving the right problem.
•	Key Details & Data: Define the user problem, desired product outcome, success criteria, and evidence needed before development.
•	Real-World Example: AI teams should validate the workflow problem before investing in a model.
Stage Gates
•	Core Concept: Each lifecycle stage should have explicit evidence required to proceed.
•	Key Details & Data: A gate can result in launch, pause, redesign, narrower rollout, or rollback.
•	Real-World Example: The session's AI Launch Gate combines quality, fallback, governance, and operational evidence.
________________________________________
DATA & PIPELINE GATE
Data Readiness
•	Core Concept: Development should not proceed merely because data exists.
•	Key Details & Data: Teams must verify signal quality, freshness, missing data, skew, and pipeline reliability.
•	Real-World Example: DoorDash ETA pipelines illustrate the importance of production signal quality.
Pipeline Health
•	Core Concept: The production system must receive signals of comparable quality to those used during development.
•	Key Details & Data: A pipeline health view should expose freshness, missing data, skew, and fragile handoffs.
•	Real-World Example: DoorDash ETA pipelines.
________________________________________
VALIDATION GATE — MODEL DEVELOPMENT & EVALUATION
Model Evaluation
•	Core Concept: Offline model improvement does not automatically translate into product improvement.
•	Key Details & Data: Evaluate baseline comparison, threshold rationale, segment performance, and launch-readiness criteria.
•	Real-World Example: Netflix ranking experiments and Airbnb search-quality evaluation.
Threshold Decisions
•	Core Concept: The PM must determine what error profile is acceptable in the actual business context.
•	Key Details & Data: Thresholds should reflect business cost, user friction, safety, and who absorbs the cost of errors.
•	Real-World Example: Netflix ranking and Airbnb search illustrate model-quality decisions tied to user outcomes.
Offline vs Real-World Performance
•	Core Concept: Offline gains can disappear when the model meets real users, operational constraints, and policy requirements.
•	Key Details & Data: Segment performance and workflow impact matter alongside technical metrics.
•	Real-World Example: A ranking model may perform well offline but fail to create better search or recommendation outcomes in production.
________________________________________
LAUNCH GATE — DEPLOYMENT & INTEGRATION
Production Deployment
•	Core Concept: A production-ready model is not necessarily a production-ready product.
•	Key Details & Data: The surrounding workflow, UI, integration, fallback, and operational process must also be ready.
•	Real-World Example: Google Maps live-route deployment and Klarna support-assistant rollout.
Workflow Integration
•	Core Concept: Decide exactly how the AI enters the user's workflow.
•	Key Details & Data: Choose among assistant, inline suggestion, background automation, or no AI. Use workflow wireframes and usability observations.
•	Real-World Example: Notion AI.
Rollback and Fallback
•	Core Concept: Teams should design recovery before launch.
•	Key Details & Data: Evidence should include rollback readiness, UI clarity, manual override paths, and post-launch incident rates.
•	Real-World Example: Google Maps ETA illustrates the importance of fallback behaviour when confidence is weak.
Quick Recall
Model Ready ≠ Product Ready
________________________________________
LIFECYCLE DELIVERABLES & OPERATING CADENCE
Experiment Brief
•	Core Concept: Defines the experiment, hypothesis, evidence, and decision before execution.
•	Key Details & Data: It creates a reusable handoff rather than relying on chats or memory.
•	Real-World Example: Ranking systems can use experiment briefs before model or product changes.
PRD and Success Criteria
•	Core Concept: Define what must be true before launch and what signals should trigger action afterward.
•	Key Details & Data: Establish rollback, redesign, or narrower-rollout criteria before launch.
•	Real-World Example: Launch criteria should be explicit before release pressure begins.
Model Card
•	Core Concept: A lifecycle artifact that makes model behaviour and launch considerations visible and traceable.
•	Key Details & Data: Used alongside launch checklists and post-launch monitoring.
•	Real-World Example: Model cards are highlighted for regulated AI workflows.
Launch Checklist
•	Core Concept: Confirms that the required product, model, governance, fallback, and operational conditions are ready.
•	Key Details & Data: Prevents teams from treating model readiness as total product readiness.
•	Real-World Example: Ranking systems can combine experiment briefs with launch checklists.
Drift Dashboard
•	Core Concept: Tracks whether the AI product's data or model behaviour changes after deployment.
•	Key Details & Data: Watch segment-level drift, incident recurrence, retraining lag, and infrastructure saturation.
•	Real-World Example: Drift dashboards are especially important in regulated AI workflows.
Human-Review Policy
•	Core Concept: Defines when decisions should be automated, assisted, or manually reviewed.
•	Key Details & Data: Track queue load, override rate, time-to-resolution, confidence bands, reviewer responsibility, and routing thresholds.
•	Real-World Example: Stripe Radar review queues.
Minimum Artifact Stack
Experiment Brief → Launch Checklist/Model Card → Drift Review
The purpose is speed + traceability + ownership, not paperwork.
________________________________________
RESILIENCE UNDER CHANGE
Model Drift
•	Core Concept: Model performance or usefulness can deteriorate as real-world conditions change.
•	Key Details & Data: Teams need signals that trigger retraining, redesign, rollback, or narrower deployment.
•	Real-World Example: Fraud models drifting during festival seasons.
Data Drift
•	Core Concept: The characteristics of incoming data can change over time.
•	Key Details & Data: Monitor event quality, completeness, freshness, and changes at segment level.
•	Real-World Example: Spotify event instrumentation is used to illustrate ongoing signal monitoring.
Infrastructure Bottlenecks
•	Core Concept: AI product performance can deteriorate because of infrastructure constraints even when the model remains sound.
•	Key Details & Data: Monitor launch gates, post-launch checks, drift alerts, response ownership, and infrastructure saturation.
•	Real-World Example: GitHub Copilot rollout discipline.
Detecting Failure Early
•	Core Concept: Intervention should happen before customers experience a major quality collapse.
•	Key Details & Data: Key evidence includes segment-level drift, incident recurrence, retraining lag, and infrastructure saturation.
•	Real-World Example: Ride-hailing ETAs degrading during weather shocks.
Drift Response
•	Core Concept: A mature product defines its response before drift occurs.
•	Key Details & Data: Use four steps: Detection → Escalation → Mitigation → Communication.
•	Real-World Example: Fraud-model changes during festival seasons can trigger monitoring and intervention.
________________________________________
OLA ETA LIFECYCLE CASE
Ola ETA Prediction
•	Core Concept: A simple customer-facing prediction may depend on a complex lifecycle behind the scenes.
•	Key Details & Data: ETA quality depends on data collection, experimentation, deployment, and continuous monitoring. Drift and changing context can weaken performance after launch.
•	Real-World Example: Ola ETA prediction demonstrates why lifecycle management continues after deployment.
Session 7 Takeaway
Deployment is not completion. It is the start of accountable operation.
Lifecycle management requires:
Problem → Data → Experiment → Model → Validation → Launch → Monitor → Detect Drift → Escalate → Improve/Rollback
The PDF describes lifecycle management as an operating system rather than a sequence of milestones.
