TERMS TO MEMORIZE
•	Product-Market Fit (PMF): Evidence that an AI product creates repeatable, trusted, economically meaningful user value.
•	Novelty Signal: Initial usage driven by curiosity rather than durable product value.
•	Workflow Dependence: Degree to which users rely on a product to complete recurring work.
•	Platform Product: Product supporting reusable capabilities across multiple jobs, users, or ecosystem participants.
•	Feature Trap: Treating a narrow AI capability as a platform without evidence of broader reusable value.
•	Recommendation System: AI system that ranks or selects options to influence discovery and behaviour.
•	Data Advantage: Competitive benefit created by valuable, reliable, difficult-to-replicate data.
•	Workflow Embedding: Integrating AI directly into an important user task or process.
•	Signal: Data that meaningfully represents the behaviour or outcome the AI must understand.
•	Event Instrumentation: Capturing defined product events for analysis or AI use.
•	Data Readiness: Degree to which data is usable, complete, fresh, representative, and reliable.
•	Data Pipeline: System that moves data from its source to downstream product or model use.
•	Data Freshness: How current the available data is for the required decision.
•	Human-in-the-Loop: Design where humans review, approve, correct, or escalate selected AI decisions.
•	Confidence: Degree of certainty associated with an AI output.
•	Fallback: Safe alternative behaviour when AI cannot provide an acceptable result.
•	Stage Gate: Evidence checkpoint used to decide whether a product moves to the next lifecycle stage.
•	Threshold: Decision boundary determining how model output is acted upon.
•	Model Card: Artifact documenting model behaviour and relevant operating considerations.
•	Launch Checklist: Artifact confirming readiness before production deployment.
•	Model Drift: Change that weakens model performance or usefulness over time.
•	Data Drift: Change in incoming data patterns relative to those expected by the system.
•	Rollback: Reversing or withdrawing a deployed change when required conditions fail.
•	Override Rate: Frequency with which human reviewers change AI decisions.
•	Escalation Trigger: Predefined signal requiring intervention or higher-level review.
