SESSION 9 — AI PRODUCT DEVELOPMENT CYCLE
Stages in development
•	Core Concept: Discovery validates the problem; concept design frames the solution; prototype tests assumptions; pilot tests real workflow value; scale requires repeatable evidence.
•	Key Details & Data: Set an explicit gate before increasing investment.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Figma-to-production copilots and Notion AI.
Types of prototypes
•	Core Concept: Concept, UX, data and workflow prototypes answer different questions.
•	Key Details & Data: Choose the least expensive artifact that resolves the most important uncertainty.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Chatbot mockups test interaction; demand forecasting data prototypes test signal quality.
Prototype versus MVP
•	Core Concept: A prototype tests a hypothesis; an MVP delivers limited real value to actual users.
•	Key Details & Data: Real exposure introduces operational cost, trust and accountability.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Early copilots tested with restricted cohorts.
MVP types and staged automation
•	Core Concept: Wizard of Oz uses manual simulation; concierge provides manual service; partial automation automates selected steps; workflow assistants support users.
•	Key Details & Data: Make manual effort and limitations visible; measure the real cost before expanding.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Concierge B2B services and staged document processing.
Development governance
•	Core Concept: Prototype briefs, scope memos, evidence reviews and go/no-go/iterate decisions preserve learning.
•	Key Details & Data: Record assumptions, thresholds and owners before the next funding decision.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: No-code AI sales copilot case: validate workflow before full integration.
Session reflection and managerial application
I would treat the first ReconPilot demonstration as a workflow prototype, not proof of market fit. A limited pilot with synthetic or approved data should follow only after users can understand recommendations and correct errors.

 SESSION 10 — EXPERIMENTATION, METRICS AND EVALUATION
AI metrics versus traditional metrics
•	Core Concept: AI quality is probabilistic and varies across segments; offline scores do not guarantee online value.
•	Key Details & Data: Compare against a baseline and monitor business outcomes and guardrails.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Digital payments fraud detection.
Classification metrics
•	Core Concept: Precision = TP/(TP+FP); recall = TP/(TP+FN); F1 = 2PR/(P+R); accuracy = (TP+TN)/total.
•	Key Details & Data: Class imbalance can make accuracy misleading. Select thresholds based on error costs.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Fraud screening balances missed fraud against legitimate transactions blocked.
Business evaluation
•	Core Concept: Revenue, conversion, error cost, trust and satisfaction complement model metrics.
•	Key Details & Data: A metric improvement is not valuable if review costs or customer friction increase.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Payments fraud case links loss prevention with customer trust.
A/B testing
•	Core Concept: Define a hypothesis, randomly allocate eligible users to treatment and control, and assess outcomes.
•	Key Details & Data: Check contamination, segment differences, uncertainty and guardrail breaches before rollout.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Compare a new fraud decision workflow with the existing baseline.
Feedback loops and metric design
•	Core Concept: User corrections can improve future models but may reinforce existing bias.
•	Key Details & Data: Validate feedback quality and use a small set of decision-linked metrics.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: The experimentation simulator illustrates metric trade-offs.
Session reflection and managerial application
For ReconPilot, I would prioritise precision of suggested matches, analyst resolution time and incorrect-match rate. I would compare these against manual reconciliation rather than claim success from model accuracy alone.

 SESSION 11 — SCALING AI PRODUCTS
Scale architecture
•	Core Concept: Scale includes users, requests, data, models, workflow complexity and organisational dependence.
•	Key Details & Data: Identify the first bottleneck before expanding usage.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Generative AI customer support assistant.
Latency, infrastructure and cost
•	Core Concept: Inference cost and response time affect both experience and margin.
•	Key Details & Data: Plan capacity and select the smallest model that meets quality requirements.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Support assistant routing can reserve expensive models for complex requests.
MLOps
•	Core Concept: Automated pipelines, repeatable deployment, monitoring and clear ownership sustain quality.
•	Key Details & Data: Define alert thresholds and incident responsibilities before scaling.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Production support workflows need reliable monitoring.
Model versioning
•	Core Concept: Track model versions, experiments, lineage and release approvals.
•	Key Details & Data: Maintain a tested rollback path and controlled rollout.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: A new model can be withdrawn if quality deteriorates.
API and GenAI scaling
•	Core Concept: Queues, rate limits, tenant isolation, service guarantees, caching and routing manage demand.
•	Key Details & Data: Balance fairness, latency, cost and safety under peak load.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: The support assistant case highlights fallback and routing.
Session reflection and managerial application
I would scale ReconPilot by transaction volume and exception complexity, not simply number of users. Batch processing, queue monitoring and versioned matching logic would be more important than adding a larger model prematurely.

 SESSION 12 — ETHICS, RISK AND RESPONSIBLE AI
Bias in AI products
•	Core Concept: Bias may enter through data, labels, policy or deployment workflow.
•	Key Details & Data: Evaluate segment-level outcomes and investigate disparate impact.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Hiring algorithms can reproduce historical bias.
Privacy
•	Core Concept: Collect only necessary data, define purpose, consent or other applicable basis, retention and access controls.
•	Key Details & Data: Avoid sensitive attributes unless justified and governed.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Hiring workflows illustrate the importance of defensible inputs.
Hallucinations
•	Core Concept: Generative systems may produce plausible but unsupported statements.
•	Key Details & Data: Ground outputs in reliable sources and abstain or escalate when evidence is insufficient.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: High-stakes support assistants require constrained answers.
Responsible design
•	Core Concept: Fairness, accountability, transparency, recourse and human review must be embedded in the workflow.
•	Key Details & Data: Make correction and escalation practical rather than merely documenting principles.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Hiring-support decisions need meaningful review.
Governance and risk assessment
•	Core Concept: Review boards, audit trails, incident response and controls make risk management operational.
•	Key Details & Data: Assess severity, likelihood, detectability and reversibility; decide whether to launch, constrain or pause.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Bias in hiring case illustrates why a launch may need to be delayed.
Session reflection and managerial application
For ReconPilot, I would avoid using live customer information in a classroom prototype. Suggested matches should show supporting evidence, require analyst approval and retain an audit trail. Uncertain or unsupported recommendations should remain unresolved.

 SESSION 13 — AI PRODUCT MONETIZATION AND BUSINESS MODELS
Pricing models
•	Core Concept: Subscription, usage-based, API and outcome-linked pricing capture value differently.
•	Key Details & Data: Match the pricing unit to customer value and cost variability.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: OpenAI API pricing case illustrates usage economics.
Inference economics
•	Core Concept: Token volume, compute, latency and model selection influence cost per task.
•	Key Details & Data: Reduce unnecessary context and route tasks appropriately without compromising quality.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: API-based products face variable usage costs.
Unit economics
•	Core Concept: Gross margin = (revenue − cost of revenue)/revenue; contribution subtracts relevant variable costs.
•	Key Details & Data: Include support, monitoring and review costs; assess retention and acquisition payback.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: Heavy users may generate less contribution under flat pricing.
Business model design
•	Core Concept: AI can be standalone, embedded, bundled or part of a platform.
•	Key Details & Data: Choose the commercial structure that matches workflow value and differentiation.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: An embedded AI feature can support upsell and retention.
API pricing and monetization risks
•	Core Concept: Model tiers, vendor dependence and unpredictable usage affect customer adoption and margins.
•	Key Details & Data: Stress-test light and heavy usage, vendor price changes and trust costs.
•	Real-World Example / Course Lens: OpenAI API case contrasts adoption speed with cost control.
Session reflection and managerial application
I would evaluate a subscription with transaction-volume bands for ReconPilot. Before recommending pricing, I would estimate cost per resolved break, analyst time saved and support overhead. These are proposed measures, not validated financial results.

